509
00:19:23,167 --> 00:19:24,867
- Tom Servo.
- Who cares, anybody's.

510
00:19:24,867 --> 00:19:25,900
Kill her!

511
00:19:25,900 --> 00:19:27,667
- Hit her!
- Harder!

512
00:19:27,667 --> 00:19:28,967
- [Girl] Scratch her eyes out.

513
00:19:28,967 --> 00:19:30,933
- [Arkie] Harder,
scratch her eyes out!

514
00:19:30,933 --> 00:19:32,433
- Come on, hit her!
- Hit her!

515
00:19:32,433 --> 00:19:34,500
- Harder!
- Hit her, kick her!

516
00:19:34,500 --> 00:19:36,200
- Kill her!
- Kill her!

517
00:19:36,200 --> 00:19:37,567
- Kill her!
- Hit her!

518
00:19:37,567 --> 00:19:41,267
- [Arkie] Harder and
harder, scratch her!

519
00:19:41,267 --> 00:19:44,033
- Hit her!
- Hit her, kick her!

520
00:19:44,033 --> 00:19:45,267
(screams)

521
00:19:45,267 --> 00:19:46,433
(laughter)
- Ow, right in the eye hole.

522
00:19:46,433 --> 00:19:48,833
- [Joel] Oh he's blind,
that's real funny.

523
00:19:48,833 --> 00:19:50,300
Real good, Greg.
- Kill her!

524
00:19:50,300 --> 00:19:52,067
- Come on!
- Go, yeah!

525
00:19:52,900 --> 00:19:54,733
(women yelling)

526
00:19:54,733 --> 00:19:57,133
- [Woman] Fight her, fight her!

527
00:19:57,133 --> 00:20:00,500
- [Lady] Come on,
Janie, pull her hair!

528
00:20:00,500 --> 00:20:02,000
- [Arkie] Come on, kill her!

529
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:03,333
(women yelling)

530
00:20:03,333 --> 00:20:04,900
- [Joel] Alright,
I'll break it up.

531
00:20:04,900 --> 00:20:06,800
Oh. Sorry, excuse me.
- Who's the big winner?

532
00:20:06,800 --> 00:20:07,633
I got winner.

533
00:20:07,633 --> 00:20:09,100
Come on, I'm ready.

534
00:20:09,100 --> 00:20:10,033
- Don't hit me in
the mouth again,

535
00:20:10,033 --> 00:20:12,167
you'll break my dental plate.

536
00:20:12,167 --> 00:20:14,233
- This what you
call entertainment?

537
00:20:14,233 --> 00:20:16,433
- Well you missed the
big Cossack number.

538
00:20:16,433 --> 00:20:18,167
- You take a different
bed or a broken plate,

539
00:20:18,167 --> 00:20:19,733
now what do you say?

540
00:20:19,733 --> 00:20:21,733
(breathes heavily)
- I say uncle.

541
00:20:21,733 --> 00:20:23,567
- [Joel] Don't say it.

542
00:20:24,700 --> 00:20:26,033
- [Tom] I say wow.

543
00:20:26,033 --> 00:20:27,533
(chuckles)

544
00:20:27,533 --> 00:20:31,133
- I advise you two to save
your energy for the fields.

545
00:20:31,133 --> 00:20:32,367
You hurt?

546
00:20:32,367 --> 00:20:34,200
(breathing heavy)

547
00:20:34,200 --> 00:20:35,033
You?

548
00:20:36,300 --> 00:20:37,567
- I don't know what
he expects from them,

549
00:20:37,567 --> 00:20:38,767
they're untamed!
- Angelo, get the

550
00:20:38,767 --> 00:20:40,833
rest of these people
onto the chow.

551
00:20:40,833 --> 00:20:42,433
- [Angelo] Alright
kids, get dressed.

552
00:20:42,433 --> 00:20:43,400
Everybody to chow.

553
00:20:43,400 --> 00:20:46,500
(women murmuring)

554
00:20:46,500 --> 00:20:48,500
- Now what was this
catfight all about?

555
00:20:48,500 --> 00:20:50,567
- Don't call me a cat.

556
00:20:50,567 --> 00:20:51,767
- Well you got a wild's temper,

557
00:20:51,767 --> 00:20:53,067
- Meow.
- I'd say.

558
00:20:54,100 --> 00:20:55,800
I asked a question.

559
00:20:55,800 --> 00:20:57,233
- It was over my bed.

560
00:20:57,233 --> 00:20:59,333
- It was not--
- Over your bed body.

561
00:20:59,333 --> 00:21:00,567
(chuckles)

562
00:21:00,567 --> 00:21:03,767
- Alright, that's
enough of this nonsense.

563
00:21:03,767 --> 00:21:06,267
Remember what I
told you, Lillibet.

564
00:21:06,267 --> 00:21:08,300
You're halfway back to
the pokey right now.

565
00:21:08,300 --> 00:21:10,800
- [Joel] What about the Gumby?

566
00:21:14,800 --> 00:21:16,067
- Cotton pickin' creep.

567
00:21:16,067 --> 00:21:17,933
- Oh, save your energy.

568
00:21:17,933 --> 00:21:19,633
He's all business.

569
00:21:19,633 --> 00:21:21,400
- Yeah, all kinds.

570
00:21:21,400 --> 00:21:23,567
Including shady, fishy,

571
00:21:23,567 --> 00:21:24,800
funny.

572
00:21:24,800 --> 00:21:27,300
- Dopey, Sneezy, Grumpy.
- And monkey business.

573
00:21:27,300 --> 00:21:28,700
- I guess if we
- Did I get them all?

574
00:21:28,700 --> 00:21:29,733
- wanna fight we will, huh?
- You missed a few,

575
00:21:29,733 --> 00:21:31,400
but that's okay.
- Oh, no.

576
00:21:31,400 --> 00:21:33,367
You're too tough for me.

577
00:21:33,367 --> 00:21:34,667
Come on, let's go

578
00:21:34,667 --> 00:21:35,333
to chow kid.
- Oh, we've gotta get out

579
00:21:35,333 --> 00:21:36,533
of here now.

580
00:21:36,533 --> 00:21:37,333
- Come on, Penny.
- Let's go eat

581
00:21:37,333 --> 00:21:38,400
at the Mess Hall, huh?

582
00:21:38,400 --> 00:21:39,900
Would you like that?
- Bleh.

583
00:21:39,900 --> 00:21:40,733
- Wait a minute,

584
00:21:40,733 --> 00:21:42,767
I gotta get dressed!

585
00:21:42,767 --> 00:21:46,567
(doors slamming and whirring)

586
00:21:51,700 --> 00:21:52,800
- Hey are you ready, you guys?

587
00:21:52,800 --> 00:21:54,100
- Ready.
- Yep.

588
00:21:54,100 --> 00:21:56,267
- Okay, today we'd like
to present our pageant,

589
00:21:56,267 --> 00:21:58,900
Greg Brady, An American Legacy.

590
00:21:58,900 --> 00:22:00,467
(humming)

591
00:22:00,467 --> 00:22:02,733
- Greg Brady, the eldest
of Mike Brady's three boys

592
00:22:02,733 --> 00:22:05,200
began his public
life at the age of 14

593
00:22:05,200 --> 00:22:07,867
when his widower father
married widow Carol,

594
00:22:07,867 --> 00:22:11,000
who was bringing up
three very lovely girls.

595
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:12,500
- He lived with his large family

596
00:22:12,500 --> 00:22:14,600
in a very spacious,
split-level house

597
00:22:14,600 --> 00:22:16,167
in a Los Angeles suburb.

598
00:22:16,167 --> 00:22:18,133
He attended Warren G.
Harding high school,

599
00:22:18,133 --> 00:22:20,967
where he was considered dreamy.

600
00:22:20,967 --> 00:22:23,967
- Life wasn't all peaches
and cream for Greg Brady.

601
00:22:23,967 --> 00:22:26,833
The pressures of a
huge step-family often

602
00:22:26,833 --> 00:22:28,733
caused things to go wrong.

603
00:22:28,733 --> 00:22:30,667
Sharing a bedroom with
two brothers would

604
00:22:30,667 --> 00:22:32,033
cramp Greg's style.

605
00:22:32,033 --> 00:22:34,267
He later proceeded
to move into the den

606
00:22:34,267 --> 00:22:35,767
and made it very mod.

607
00:22:35,767 --> 00:22:37,033
(hums)

608
00:22:37,033 --> 00:22:38,433
- He wasn't ready
for the den life,

609
00:22:38,433 --> 00:22:40,733
so he moved back to the
security of his brothers' room.

610
00:22:40,733 --> 00:22:42,767
But again, he felt crowded
and he eventually settled

611
00:22:42,767 --> 00:22:43,867
in the attic.

612
00:22:45,267 --> 00:22:46,967
- The attic was good for Greg.

613
00:22:46,967 --> 00:22:49,400
There, he blossomed into a man.

614
00:22:49,400 --> 00:22:51,567
Nurturing his singing
career, just trying to forget

615
00:22:51,567 --> 00:22:53,267
the troubles of growing up.

616
00:22:53,267 --> 00:22:55,867
Like the time his hair
got turned orange.

617
00:22:55,867 --> 00:22:57,867
- Or when Bobby left
his jumping frog

618
00:22:57,867 --> 00:22:59,800
in the back of Greg's car

619
00:22:59,800 --> 00:23:00,900
when he had a date.

620
00:23:00,900 --> 00:23:02,367
Oh, the carnage.

621
00:23:02,367 --> 00:23:04,833
Anyway, but that
was his past life.

622
00:23:04,833 --> 00:23:06,200
Life was good now.

623
00:23:06,200 --> 00:23:10,367
In 1971, Greg was
receiving 6,500 fan letters

624
00:23:10,367 --> 00:23:11,833
a week.

625
00:23:11,833 --> 00:23:14,633
That and his studies at
Harding high kept Greg busy.

626
00:23:14,633 --> 00:23:15,867
(hums)

627
00:23:15,867 --> 00:23:17,833
- Then in 1974, Greg was
faced with the biggest

628
00:23:17,833 --> 00:23:19,200
shock of his life.

629
00:23:19,200 --> 00:23:21,433
His family was canceled.

630
00:23:21,433 --> 00:23:22,600
Without a primetime audience,

631
00:23:22,600 --> 00:23:24,800
Greg's world began to crumble.

632
00:23:24,800 --> 00:23:27,000
- There were attempts
to revive his family,

633
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:28,500
but none succeeded.

634
00:23:28,500 --> 00:23:30,033
He had hit rock bottom.

635
00:23:30,033 --> 00:23:33,733
Greg couldn't even get a
suite on The Love Boat.

636
00:23:33,733 --> 00:23:35,767
- Brady lunchbox in hand,

637
00:23:35,767 --> 00:23:37,733
Greg headed for New York.

638
00:23:37,733 --> 00:23:40,433
Musical theater would
give him a last chance.

639
00:23:40,433 --> 00:23:43,300
He appeared in the Broadway
production of Pippin,

640
00:23:43,300 --> 00:23:44,400
but then disappeared.

641
00:23:44,400 --> 00:23:45,767
(hums)

642
00:23:45,767 --> 00:23:48,300
- It seemed America
had lost Greg Brady.

643
00:23:48,300 --> 00:23:49,833
He was nowhere to be found.

644
00:23:49,833 --> 00:23:50,767
Until...

645
00:23:50,767 --> 00:23:52,100
Christmas, 1988.

646
00:23:53,367 --> 00:23:56,200
- Greg was found at a
hospital in California.

647
00:23:56,200 --> 00:23:57,167
It was him!

648
00:23:57,167 --> 00:23:58,500
It was Doctor Greg Brady!

649
00:23:58,500 --> 00:24:01,967
He had become a
doctor Greg Brady.

650
00:24:01,967 --> 00:24:03,933
- He returned to his boy home--

651
00:24:03,933 --> 00:24:06,133
boyhood home with
his entire family

652
00:24:06,133 --> 00:24:08,667
and a Very Brady
Christmas was had by all.

653
00:24:08,667 --> 00:24:09,833
Thank you.

654
00:24:09,833 --> 00:24:10,767
What?

655
00:24:10,767 --> 00:24:12,033
Go ahead.
- Greg Brady.

656
00:24:12,033 --> 00:24:14,833
We on the Satellite
of Love salute you.

657
00:24:14,833 --> 00:24:16,533
- You're one of the good ones.

658
00:24:16,533 --> 00:24:17,533
- Thank you, Greg.

659
00:24:17,533 --> 00:24:20,800
You are groovy. (hums)

660
00:24:23,567 --> 00:24:27,233
("Love Theme Instrumental")

661
00:24:30,933 --> 00:24:33,767
(motorcycle revving)

662
00:24:33,767 --> 00:24:35,000
- [Tom] Good pageant.

663
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:35,933
- [Joel] Yeah.

664
00:24:35,933 --> 00:24:37,733
I thought it was pretty good.

665
00:24:37,733 --> 00:24:40,033
- [Tom] It should be
just on what I love.

666
00:24:40,033 --> 00:24:42,700
- [Joel] Hey, it's Evel Knievel.

667
00:24:47,300 --> 00:24:48,133
- Whatcha got Buster?

668
00:24:48,133 --> 00:24:49,100
Telegram?

669
00:24:49,100 --> 00:24:49,967
Alright.

670
00:24:49,967 --> 00:24:50,800
- Mr. Tropp?

671
00:24:50,800 --> 00:24:51,633
- Right.

672
00:24:51,633 --> 00:24:52,700
- Bob Steele.

673
00:24:53,633 --> 00:24:55,367
- Hm?
- Well, I'm Rod Iron.

674
00:24:55,367 --> 00:24:56,900
- How are you, glad to meet you.

675
00:24:56,900 --> 00:24:58,000
Your mother called me about you.

676
00:24:58,000 --> 00:24:59,200
Say, you sure look like her.

677
00:24:59,200 --> 00:25:00,533
- Thanks,

678
00:25:00,533 --> 00:25:01,200
- She tells me you run
- I'm taking steroids.

679
00:25:01,200 --> 00:25:02,467
- a harvester.

680
00:25:02,467 --> 00:25:03,667
Haven't forgotten how?
- Nope, I can run one.

681
00:25:03,667 --> 00:25:04,933
- Good, you're on the
payroll as of now.

682
00:25:04,933 --> 00:25:06,167
Regular rate, and
glad to have you.

683
00:25:06,167 --> 00:25:07,667
- Well thank you very much, sir.

684
00:25:07,667 --> 00:25:09,800
- [Joel] Now I don't know
if you're into weird scenes

685
00:25:09,800 --> 00:25:12,067
or not, but check
this thing out.

686
00:25:12,067 --> 00:25:13,233
- I grew up here.

687
00:25:13,233 --> 00:25:14,633
- How's the place look to you?

688
00:25:14,633 --> 00:25:15,867
- Swell.

689
00:25:15,867 --> 00:25:17,200
Gee, you really
have it fixed up.

690
00:25:17,200 --> 00:25:18,867
- I really like your dirt.
- Looks great.

691
00:25:18,867 --> 00:25:20,367
- Takes money, though.

692
00:25:20,367 --> 00:25:22,533
The easiest part was getting
your mother to sell out to me.

693
00:25:22,533 --> 00:25:23,733
- Yeah, I know.

694
00:25:23,733 --> 00:25:24,933
- She does that a lot.
- After Pop died,

695
00:25:24,933 --> 00:25:26,867
she got so she hated
it around here.

696
00:25:26,867 --> 00:25:29,100
She wrote me in Formosa
she was gonna sell out.

697
00:25:29,100 --> 00:25:29,933
- Landis!

698
00:25:31,467 --> 00:25:33,400
(chugging)

699
00:25:33,400 --> 00:25:34,233
- That's Gladys?
- Come down, I got a

700
00:25:34,233 --> 00:25:35,700
job for ya.

701
00:25:35,700 --> 00:25:36,633
- But I was just going

702
00:25:36,633 --> 00:25:38,533
to the store.
- Come on.

703
00:25:44,367 --> 00:25:46,133
Bob Steele, this is Jack Landis.

704
00:25:46,133 --> 00:25:47,333
- Hi.

705
00:25:47,333 --> 00:25:48,567
- Bob's gonna take
over your job.

706
00:25:48,567 --> 00:25:51,667
I want you to handle the
field crew on Q ranch.

707
00:25:51,667 --> 00:25:53,533
- I'll go get my gear.

708
00:25:53,533 --> 00:25:54,833
- [Joel] Oh great,
first guy I meet

709
00:25:54,833 --> 00:25:55,767
hates me.
- You can stay in the

710
00:25:55,767 --> 00:25:56,633
Foreman's bunkhouse.

711
00:25:56,633 --> 00:25:57,800
- Fine.

712
00:25:57,800 --> 00:25:59,267
- Now there's cooking
facilities there

713
00:25:59,267 --> 00:26:00,300
or you can eat with the
crew down at the Mess Hall.

714
00:26:00,300 --> 00:26:01,100
- Okay.

715
00:26:04,100 --> 00:26:05,533
- Landis.

716
00:26:05,533 --> 00:26:06,367
Come here.

717
00:26:10,567 --> 00:26:11,800
- [Joel] Salad oil?

718
00:26:11,800 --> 00:26:12,767
- Get rid of that.

719
00:26:12,767 --> 00:26:14,633
- I'll drink it.
- And Landis.

720
00:26:14,633 --> 00:26:15,800
- [Tom] That's no way to pack

721
00:26:15,800 --> 00:26:17,033
a reserve shoot.
- Guzzling on the job

722
00:26:17,033 --> 00:26:19,633
again, you'll be off of
here in five minutes.

723
00:26:19,633 --> 00:26:21,133
Go join your crew.

724
00:26:24,667 --> 00:26:25,500
Okay, kid.

725
00:26:26,467 --> 00:26:27,667
On your way.

726
00:26:27,667 --> 00:26:28,900
(chugging)

727
00:26:28,900 --> 00:26:30,233
- [Joel] Man, that
kids sure looks good

728
00:26:30,233 --> 00:26:32,000
up on that combine.

729
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,533
Put some spurs on him,
make him sing show tunes.

730
00:26:34,533 --> 00:26:36,667
He'll fit in real fine.

731
00:26:36,667 --> 00:26:39,267
(dreamy music)

732
00:26:39,267 --> 00:26:40,833
- Nature's rich bounty.

733
00:26:40,833 --> 00:26:44,467
Corn, soy beans,
sore gums, spackum.

734
00:26:44,467 --> 00:26:46,767
Mining, agriculture,
and untamed youth

735
00:26:46,767 --> 00:26:50,033
forging an untamed land
eating their weight in cotton.

736
00:26:50,033 --> 00:26:51,633
- [Tom] Men naked
from the waist up

737
00:26:51,633 --> 00:26:54,400
watching Days of Our
Lives, day after day.

738
00:26:54,400 --> 00:26:56,367
This is their story, the
valiant few who dared

739
00:26:56,367 --> 00:26:58,767
to be different,
who dared to dream.

740
00:26:58,767 --> 00:27:00,733
- [Joel] Monster cars
patrolling the highways

741
00:27:00,733 --> 00:27:01,633
and byways.

742
00:27:02,833 --> 00:27:04,333
- [Radio] But in spite
of this bumbered crop,

743
00:27:04,333 --> 00:27:06,700
prices may continue to rise.

744
00:27:06,700 --> 00:27:09,000
Why? Because of the
acute shortage of labor

745
00:27:09,000 --> 00:27:12,367
in many parts of the Southwest.

746
00:27:12,367 --> 00:27:14,933
- Hey, he's got a car payphone.

747
00:27:18,233 --> 00:27:20,267
- Howdy, Mr. Tropp.

748
00:27:20,267 --> 00:27:21,333
- [Russ] Hiya.

749
00:27:23,800 --> 00:27:25,967
(imitates horn)

750
00:27:25,967 --> 00:27:27,133
- I love that gag.

751
00:27:27,133 --> 00:27:28,300
- Yancey.

752
00:27:28,300 --> 00:27:29,533
Collingwood.

753
00:27:29,533 --> 00:27:31,367
- Well, Mr. Tropp.

754
00:27:31,367 --> 00:27:32,700
Looks like you're
gonna have your crops

755
00:27:32,700 --> 00:27:35,833
picked clean and
baled in short order.

756
00:27:35,833 --> 00:27:38,467
We just happen to notice
going by your field.

757
00:27:38,467 --> 00:27:40,167
You got plenty of
hands workin' for you?

758
00:27:40,167 --> 00:27:41,600
- That's right.
- Two per person.

759
00:27:41,600 --> 00:27:42,833
- Picking cotton.

760
00:27:42,833 --> 00:27:44,467
- Our crops are ready and ripe.

761
00:27:44,467 --> 00:27:46,100
Soon they'll be rotten.

762
00:27:46,100 --> 00:27:47,233
- I made you an offer.
- Rotten cotton.

763
00:27:47,233 --> 00:27:48,733
- It's your move.

764
00:27:48,733 --> 00:27:50,900
- You said you could get us
labor to pick our fields.

765
00:27:50,900 --> 00:27:52,167
- That's right.

766
00:27:52,167 --> 00:27:54,500
I take 60% of your
harvest, you get 40.

767
00:27:54,500 --> 00:27:56,233
